//! member: true
//!
//! std.x.option_expect — `std.option`'s assert-on-absence helper, parked
//! outside the facade for one missing type. Stability tier: x (nursery,
//! D31 clock: it graduates INTO `std.option` verbatim, or is deleted, at
//! the stdc02 closeout).
//!
//! Why it is here and not in `std.option`: the handler traps, so it
//! produces no value — but wolfc types an `else |_| { … }` block by its
//! last expression, `assert(false)` is `()`, and the block must produce
//! `T` (`E0401: this is (), but the else fallback must produce T`). A
//! MONOMORPHIC helper works around it with an unreachable dummy after the
//! trap; a generic one has no `T` to conjure, and conjuring one is
//! precisely what a bottom type (`never`, `!`) exists for. wolf does not
//! spell one and D30's no-unwinding makes divergence ordinary rather than
//! exceptional, so the gap is filed rather than papered over (F-0040).
//! lupin accepts the diverging handler and executes it, which is where
//! the evidence below comes from.
//!
//! One rejected body poisons a module for every importer, so the cost of
//! keeping this beside `or`/`to_list`/`exists`/`is_none` would be their
//! static ladder. It moves back on the pin that gives the language a
//! bottom type or gives `else` a diverging-handler rule.

/// `v`'s value, or a trap carrying `msg` when `v` is absent — the
/// assertion form of absence handling, for a miss the caller has already
/// ruled out by construction.
///
/// Rows: consumes `none`.
/// Traps `assert` (`[conf.trap.map]`) on the miss, after printing one
/// `FAILED: {msg}` line, exactly as `testing.fail` does. It never returns
/// normally on a miss: a trap ends the process (D30, no unwinding), which
/// is why this is the wrong tool for data-driven absence and the right
/// one for an invariant.
///
/// Example (prose — a row value has no literal, F-0038; runnable in
/// `tests/x/option_expect/expect_trap.lu`):
/// `option_expect.expect(find(5), "5 is positive")` is `5`;
/// `option_expect.expect(find(0), "0 is positive")` prints
/// `FAILED: 0 is positive` and traps `assert`.
pub fn expect[T](v: T ! {none}, msg: str) -> T {
    let hit = v else |_| {
        print("FAILED: {msg}")
        assert(false)
    }
    hit
}
